1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A verified-free route hits a synthetic 429. Paid fallback is blocked, so ZeroSpend tries another verified-free route.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:15,000
ZeroSpend is a local router that admits routes only when current evidence says they are free.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:32,000
This synthetic SQL request moves through one continuous policy decision: classify, select Route A, record temporary capacity, block paid fallback, and select Route B.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Free models change. Providers rate-limit. Catalog evidence can become stale or ambiguous, so discovery and verification are bounded and repeatable.

5
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Classification is deterministic. Code, SQL, reasoning, and tool requests map to aliases with ordered verified-free candidates.

6
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The local console shows provider evidence, route state, latency, token counts, benchmark signals, and fallback depth without storing prompt content.

7
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Try the synthetic demo locally with npm run demo. Provider availability changes over time.
